    <h2>FAQ</h2>

    <h3>Is WooCommerce or BigCommerce cheaper?</h3>
    <p>At sticker price, WooCommerce appears cheaper because the core plugin is free. However, WooCommerce requires paid WordPress hosting ($15–$100/mo for ecommerce-grade), a premium theme ($50–$200 one-time), paid plugins for cart abandonment, subscriptions, reviews, SEO, and backup, and often a developer or agency on retainer. BigCommerce Standard at $39/month includes hosting, CDN, security, PCI scope, and dozens of native features — multi-currency, B2B price lists, advanced product options, abandoned cart saver, and coupon engine — with 0% extra transaction fees. For stores between $200k and $5M per year, BigCommerce is usually the cheaper option on total cost of ownership. For stores under $50k with WordPress expertise on staff, WooCommerce can be cheaper.</p>

    <h3>Which platform is better for SEO?</h3>
    <p>WooCommerce on WordPress has a higher SEO ceiling because WordPress ships with Yoast SEO or Rank Math, which give control over every meta tag, XML sitemap, schema markup, canonical URL, breadcrumb, and redirect. You also control page speed through caching plugins, image optimization, and CDN choice. BigCommerce has strong built-in SEO — clean URLs, automatic structured data for products, CDN, sitemaps, and blog/blog-to-product linking — but you cannot dive into technical SEO details like redirect managers or page-level schema customization at the same depth. For content-heavy stores that win on SEO, WooCommerce wins. For stores that need solid SEO out of the box without tuning, BigCommerce is fine.</p>

    <h3>Can I migrate from WooCommerce to BigCommerce?</h3>
    <p>Yes, and many seven-figure WooCommerce stores do so when WordPress plugin bloat and hosting costs become prohibitive. BigCommerce offers a free migration assistant that imports products, customers, and orders from WooCommerce via its WordPress connector. For stores under 10,000 SKUs, the automated migration handles 80–90% of data. Theme migration is manual because WooCommerce themes are PHP/WordPress-based while BigCommerce uses Stencil/Handlebars templates — budget 2–4 weeks for a new Stencil theme. Custom plugins, payment workflows, and membership sites require re-implementation using BigCommerce apps, the Script Manager, or APIs. Most merchants run both platforms in parallel for 30–60 days before cutting over.</p>

    <h3>Which platform has better B2B features?</h3>
    <p>BigCommerce has stronger native B2B features. B2B Edition (on Pro and Enterprise) ships with customer-specific price lists, quote requests, purchase order payment, account hierarchies, company users with roles, net terms, and punchout/cXML integrations. WooCommerce does B2B through third-party plugins like WooCommerce B2B, Wholesale Suite, and Product Add-Ons — the ecosystem is mature but every B2B feature is paid and each plugin adds another point of failure, support burden, and compatibility risk. For pure B2B or hybrid B2C+B2B stores, BigCommerce is the stronger native choice.</p>

